ship. C's concerns: difficulty communicating, egocentric, not willing to further education. Both affect crew cooperation. C is 100% reliable and creates positive atmosphere, which is strong. D takes responsibility seriously, concentration, problem solving. Which is more important? For long-distance, reliability and crew atmosphere are crucial. D's hot temper could be dangerous under stress. C's communication difficulty could also be dangerous. Hard.\n\nWe could propose that we need more info on C's stress resilience and decision quality under pressure. But we have \"can make correct decisions quickly\" which suggests decision quality. No explicit stress resilience, but quick decisions may indicate calmness. So C seems strong on decision quality, reliability, positive atmosphere, but communication issues.\n\nWe could argue that C might be a better fit than D because his reliability and positive crew atmosphere outweigh D's temperament concerns. So we could shift vote to C. But we need to justify with evidence.\n\nLet's propose: I think Candidate C's 100% reliability and positive crew atmosphere are critical, and his ability to make correct decisions quickly shows decision quality. While his communication difficulty and egocentric attitude are concerns, they may be mitigated through training, whereas D's hot temper and arrogance could be more volatile under stress. Therefore I propose Candidate C as the leading candidate.\n\nWe must reference prior discussion: we have just discussed B's interpersonal issues, and D's strengths/concerns. Now we bring C.\n\nThus our public message: \"I appreciate the focus on crew cooperation. While D has strong problem solving, his hot temper and know-it-all attitude raise concerns. Candidate C, on the other hand, is noted as 100% reliable and creates a positive atmosphere, and can make correct decisions quickly, which addresses reliability and decision quality. His communication difficulty and egocentric style are concerns, but perhaps